---
/**
 * One card, in full — the body of `/card/<slug>`, and the thing `docs/PLAN.md`
 * Phase 2 says people arrive for.
 *
 * "Every view is a URL. `/card/command-and-conquer` … is the product, not
 * decoration on it." So this page is built to be *pasted*: the name is the
 * heading, the legality is dated and evidenced, and everything on it is either
 * a value read off the upstream record or a fixed label wrapped around one.
 *
 * SIX DECISIONS, EACH OF WHICH A REVIEWER SHOULD BE ABLE TO ARGUE WITH.
 *
 * **THE VERDICT IS A LIST OF PILLS, NEVER A SINGLE WORD, AND IT IS NEVER
 * `cc_legal`.** `apps/site/src/lib/cards.ts` derives it; this page only prints
 * what it derived. The distinction matters because upstream's `cc_legal` means
 * "in the Classic Constructed card pool" and is `true` on all 51 `cc_banned`
 * cards — rendering that flag as a Legal pill is precisely the incumbent bug
 * `docs/PLAN.md` Phase 3 cites as the reason this project exists. One card
 * (Winter's Wail) is `cc_banned` *and* `cc_living_legend`, so both pills render.
 *
 * **EVERY VERDICT SHIPS ITS EVIDENCE, AND AN UNUSUAL ONE SHOWS IT UNASKED.**
 * The raw upstream keys sit beneath the pills in the mono voice, with an absent
 * key shown as `—` rather than as `false`. `docs/PLAN.md`: the tool is
 * "auditable rather than merely assertive, which is what matters when the
 * commercially-backed incumbent ships incorrect flags."
 *
 * This comment used to end "it is not behind a disclosure widget, because a
 * claim whose evidence needs a click is a claim being asserted", and the
 * evidence is now behind one — so the sentence is corrected rather than left to
 * contradict the code beneath it. The argument it made is not abandoned: see
 * {@link evidenceOpen}, where `open` is computed rather than chosen. Any verdict
 * carrying a real exclusion — banned, suspended, restricted, Living Legend —
 * arrives already open. What folds is the verdict nobody would dispute.
 *
 * **THE PAGE MAKES NO HISTORICAL CLAIM.** Every legality sentence here is
 * present-tense and stamped with the date the corpus was confirmed. `since`
 * dates are printed where upstream publishes one and are labelled as the start
 * of the *current* state, never as a history. Legality as of a date is Phase 3
 * and is named as absent rather than implied.
 *
 * **THE STAT BLOCK IS A `<dl>`.** Cost, Power, Defence, Life, Intellect and
 * Arcane are term-and-value data, so they are marked up as term-and-value data
 * rather than as a row of styled `<div>`s. A card with no power has no Power
 * row at all — and 13 cards genuinely have `power: "0"`, which is a different
 * fact and gets a row saying `0`.
 *
 * **EVERY LINK TO ANOTHER CARD RENDERS `link.label`, NEVER `link.name`.** 900
 * names in this corpus belong to two to four different cards, so a list of
 * anchors reading "Head Jab", "Head Jab", "Head Jab" pointing at three URLs is
 * both useless and a WCAG 2.4.4 failure. The label carries the variant suffix
 * and is composed once, in `cards.ts`, so a page cannot forget it.
 *
 * **THE FACE IS THE FIRST THING, AND IT ARRIVES THROUGH A PRIMITIVE.** This
 * comment used to say the opposite — that no face was rendered, deliberately,
 * because card images are permitted only on the condition that a copyright line
 * accompanies them and the primitive designed to make that condition
 * unrepresentable-if-missing did not exist yet. It exists now. `CardFace` emits
 * `CARD_IMAGE_COPYRIGHT` itself on every render, with no prop and no variant
 * that can drop it, so the condition is satisfied by construction rather than by
 * this page remembering. The printings table still cites collector number, set
 * and artist, because that is the citable identity of a printing and a picture
 * is not a citation.
 *
 * **THE RAIL SHOWS ONLY PRINTINGS THAT HAVE ART.** A card averages 3.3
 * printings and several list a cold-foil variant upstream does not publish a
 * face for. A row of NO IMAGE tiles would be a worse artefact than a shorter
 * rail, and the table below still lists every printing, so nothing is hidden.
 *
 * Everything visible is composed from `optfall-components`. The styles below
 * are layout and voice assignment only, and every value in them is a
 * `var(--of-*)`; `bun run check:tokens` fails the build otherwise.
 */
import {
  BevelledPlate,
  CardFace,
  OrnamentalRule,
  PitchJewel,
  StatGlyph,
  StatePill,
} from "optfall-components/svelte";

import PrintingPicker from "./PrintingPicker.svelte";
import PrintedText from "./PrintedText.astro";
import { symbolForKind, type SymbolKind } from "../lib/card-symbols";

import {
  CORPUS,
  LAST_CONFIRMED,
  hrefForSlug,
  type CardLink,
  type CardPage,
} from "../lib/cards";
import corpusJson from "../../../../data/rules/cr-2.14.0.json";
import { buildKeywordVocabulary, rulesForCard } from "../lib/keywords";
import { hrefForNumber, type RulesCorpus } from "../lib/search";
import {
  editionName,
  foilingName,
  hrefForSet,
  rarityName,
  setName,
} from "../lib/sets";
import {
  boxFor,
  faceKeyFor,
  faceUrl,
  orientationOf,
  placeholderUrl,
} from "../lib/faces";

interface Props {
  page: CardPage;
}

const { page } = Astro.props;
const { card } = page;

const typeLine = card.type_text.trim();
const text = card.functional_text.trim();

/**
 * Lists upstream publishes per card, rendered only where non-empty.
 *
 * Read off the record and labelled, never summarised. `traits` is populated on
 * 9 cards and `removed_keywords` on 12 — printing an empty "Traits: none" row
 * on the other 4,932 would be noise dressed as completeness.
 */
const vocabularies: readonly (readonly [string, readonly string[]])[] = [
  ["Types", card.types],
  ["Traits", card.traits],
  ["Keywords", card.card_keywords],
  ["Abilities and effects", card.abilities_and_effects],
  ["Ability keywords", card.ability_and_effect_keywords],
  ["Grants", card.granted_keywords],
  ["Removes", card.removed_keywords],
  ["Interacts with", card.interacts_with_keywords],
];
const vocabulary = vocabularies.filter(([, values]) => values.length > 0);

/**
 * Which glyph each printed stat takes. Pitch is absent deliberately — the jewel
 * owns that one and its diamond is reserved to it.
 */
const GLYPH_FOR: Record<string, "cost" | "power" | "defence" | "life" | "intellect" | "arcane" | undefined> = {
  Cost: "cost",
  Power: "power",
  Defence: "defence",
  Life: "life",
  Intellect: "intellect",
  Arcane: "arcane",
};

/**
 * THE PRINTED SYMBOL FOR EACH STAT, so the stat block teaches the notation.
 *
 * A reader who meets `+1{p}` in the card text below should be able to look up
 * and see that Power is `{p}` — and after `card-symbols.ts` landed there is a
 * published table saying exactly that, so the row can carry the marker as fact
 * rather than as a hint.
 *
 * TWO ROWS DELIBERATELY GET NOTHING, and the gaps are the honest part:
 *
 *   - **Pitch** has no symbol in the rules. It has the jewel, which is the
 *     reserved silhouette and already says pitch three ways.
 *   - **Arcane** has no symbol either. CR 1.12.4 names eight and arcane is not
 *     among them, so the row shows its plate and no marker. Inventing `{a}` to
 *     make the column tidy is the exact move this file spent several phases
 *     refusing to make about `{p}`.
 *
 * Cost maps to the RESOURCE symbol, which is not a fudge: CR 1.12.4e makes
 * `{r}` a resource value, and a cost is the resource value you pay. They share
 * the hexagonal plate for the same reason.
 */
const SYMBOL_FOR: Record<string, SymbolKind | undefined> = {
  Cost: "resource",
  Power: "power",
  Defence: "defence",
  Life: "life",
  Intellect: "intellect",
};

const printedStats = page.stats.map((stat) => {
  const kind = SYMBOL_FOR[stat.label];
  return {
    label: stat.label,
    value: stat.value,
    kind: GLYPH_FOR[stat.label] ?? null,
    symbol: kind === undefined ? null : symbolForKind(kind),
  };
});

/**
 * THE STATS SPLIT ACROSS TWO BANDS, THE WAY THE THING THEY DESCRIBE DOES.
 *
 * Pitch and cost are what you pay and they ride the name; power, defence, life,
 * intellect and arcane are what the card *is* and they ride the type line. That
 * is Scryfall's arrangement — mana cost on the name line, power/toughness on
 * the type line — read onto Flesh and Blood's own stat set rather than copied
 * slot for slot, because the games do not have the same stats.
 *
 * Pitch stays IN the record rather than only in the masthead. An earlier note
 * here defended that as "a tabular record with a hole in it where its most-read
 * value should be is a worse artefact than a duplicated one", and it was right;
 * what has changed is that there is no longer a separate record for it to be
 * missing from. The bands ARE the record, and pitch appears in this one as the
 * jewel — one `<dl>` entry rendered as the reserved silhouette instead of as a
 * numeral, which is the same fact in the channel the system assigns it.
 */
const costStat = printedStats.find((stat) => stat.kind === "cost");

/**
 * WHICH CORNER EACH STAT IS PRINTED IN.
 *
 * The panel mirrors the card, so a stat goes where the card puts it: power and
 * intellect are struck into the bottom-LEFT plate, defence and life into the
 * bottom-RIGHT one. Pitch and cost are the top corners and are handled above.
 *
 * ARCANE IS THE ONE THAT IS NOT PRINTED ANYWHERE. Upstream publishes it as a
 * stat and the card does not carry it in a corner — Aether Flare's "3 arcane"
 * lives in its rules text, not on its frame. It is real data and it is shown,
 * grouped with the right-hand corner because it has to be somewhere; that is a
 * layout decision and not a claim that the card prints it there.
 */
const CORNER_FOR: Record<string, "start" | "end" | undefined> = {
  Power: "start",
  Intellect: "start",
  Defence: "end",
  Life: "end",
  Arcane: "end",
};

const bodyStats = printedStats.filter((stat) => stat.kind !== "cost");
const startStats = bodyStats.filter((stat) => CORNER_FOR[stat.label] === "start");
const endStats = bodyStats.filter((stat) => CORNER_FOR[stat.label] !== "start");
const hasStats = page.pitch !== 0 || printedStats.length > 0;

/**
 * Everyone who drew this card, once each.
 *
 * `docs/SCRYFALL-GAP.md` §5.1c: the printing is the addressable unit, and
 * attribution belongs to a printing rather than to a card — so a card with four
 * printings by three artists genuinely has three. Deduplicated in encounter
 * order rather than sorted: upstream lists printings in its own order and
 * re-sorting names would be a claim about billing that the data does not make.
 */
const artists = [...new Set(card.printings.flatMap((printing) => printing.artists))];

/**
 * The rarities this card has been printed at, once each.
 *
 * AND THERE IS GENUINELY MORE THAN ONE, on 1,259 of the 4,941 cards in the
 * corpus — a quarter of them. Rarity belongs to a printing, not to a card: the
 * same card is Common in its starter deck and Majestic in the set it debuted
 * in, and picking either one to print beside the artist would be choosing a
 * fact the data does not choose. So all of them render, deduplicated in
 * encounter order, exactly as the artists on the same line do.
 *
 * The upstream single-letter code is not what is shown. `rarityName` maps it
 * through the published decode table, and the bubble takes the NAME's first
 * letter rather than the code — they agree for nine of the ten, and the tenth
 * is `V` for "Marvel", where showing the code would print a letter that is in
 * no word on the page.
 */
const rarities = (() => {
  const seen = new Set<string>();
  const found: { name: string; initial: string; rest: string; slug: string }[] = [];

  for (const printing of card.printings) {
    if (printing.rarity === "" || seen.has(printing.rarity)) continue;
    seen.add(printing.rarity);

    const name = rarityName(printing.rarity);
    found.push({
      name,
      initial: name.slice(0, 1),
      rest: name.slice(1),
      /* "Super Rare" → `super`, which is the token family's own spelling. A
         rarity upstream adds later falls through to a name with no colour
         rather than to a broken `var()`; see `.rarity-mark` for the default. */
      slug: name.toLowerCase().split(" ")[0] ?? "",
    });
  }

  return found;
})();

/**
 * The card's flavour text, WHEN THERE IS ONE OF IT.
 *
 * Flavour is per-printing data and this page is a card, so it cannot simply be
 * shown the way Scryfall shows it — Scryfall's card page IS a printing. But
 * where every printing that carries flavour carries the SAME flavour, the
 * distinction is one the data does not draw either, and hiding a card's only
 * flavour line inside a printings appendix to preserve a distinction that has
 * collapsed is pedantry rather than precision.
 *
 * So: exactly one distinct text and it is the card's, shown in the panel where
 * a reader looks for it. Two or more and they stay in the printings section,
 * each attributed to the collector number it was printed under, because then
 * the difference is real and only that list can show it.
 */
const flavours = [
  ...new Set(
    page.printings
      .map(({ printing }) => printing.flavor_text.trim())
      .filter((flavour) => flavour !== ""),
  ),
];
const soleFlavour = flavours.length === 1 ? flavours[0] : undefined;

const nameHref = hrefForSlug(page.nameSlug);

/** Related-card lists, in the order they answer a question about this card. */
const related: readonly (readonly [string, string, readonly CardLink[]])[] = [
  ["Other versions", "Same name, different pitch.", page.variants],
  ["Names these cards", "Cards this card's text refers to by name.", page.references],
  ["Named by", "Cards whose text refers to this one by name.", page.referencedBy],
];
const relatedShown = related.filter(([, , links]) => links.length > 0);

const printingCount = card.printings.length;

/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* The rules that govern this card                                             */
/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/**
 * THE JOIN NOTHING ELSE IN THE GAME MAKES. `docs/PLAN.md` Phase 4 lists
 * "Card ↔ rules cross-reference" as a deliverable; this is it, on the surface
 * that needs it.
 *
 * Both sides are published documents and the join is exact matching over a
 * closed vocabulary the Comprehensive Rules itself defines — see `lib/keywords`
 * for the extraction and for why this survives the no-language-model rule. A
 * keyword either cites a rule the document names, or it appears without one.
 * Nothing here guesses at the nearest paragraph.
 *
 * Built at build time from the committed corpus; the 651 KB of rules text never
 * reaches a browser, only the identifiers do.
 */
const keywordRules = rulesForCard(
  buildKeywordVocabulary(corpusJson as unknown as RulesCorpus),
  [...card.card_keywords, ...card.ability_and_effect_keywords],
);

/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* The face                                                                    */
/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/**
 * The accessible name for the card's picture.
 *
 * FIXED LABELS AROUND VERBATIM VALUES, exactly as `titleFor` and
 * `descriptionFor` in `lib/cards.ts` are built — the card's own label and its
 * own printed type line, joined by an em dash this file supplies. Nothing here
 * describes the artwork, because Optfall has no way to know what is in it and a
 * generated description of a picture is precisely the composed prose the
 * project rules out. A reader who cannot see the image gets the card's identity,
 * which is what the image is doing on the page.
 */
const faceAlt = typeLine === "" ? page.label : `${page.label} — ${typeLine}`;

/*
  `faceBox` and `faceSrc` used to be computed here and were read by nothing —
  the picker resolves its own source per printing, and the no-art case below has
  `facelessBox`. Two `ts(6133)` warnings that predate this change, deleted while
  the file is open rather than left for the next person to wonder about.
*/

/**
 * Every printing that publishes art, as a rail beneath the face.
 *
 * `docs/SCRYFALL-GAP.md` §5.1c: the printing is the addressable unit, and a
 * card averaging 3.3 of them should show them rather than list their codes in a
 * table. Printings with no published art are deliberately absent from the rail
 * — a row of NO IMAGE tiles would be a worse artefact than a shorter rail, and
 * the printings TABLE below still lists every one of them, so nothing is hidden.
 */
/**
 * Every distinct printing, for the picker.
 *
 * Named by SET and number rather than by number alone: `BEN010` answers "which
 * printing" for somebody who already knows the set codes and for nobody else.
 * Deduplicated by face, because foiling variants share one image and two
 * identical tiles say nothing twice.
 */
const printings = (() => {
  const seen = new Set<string>();
  const entries: {
    key: string;
    id: string;
    edition: string;
    setName: string;
    setCode: string;
    thumb: string;
    normal: string;
    width: number;
    height: number;
    thumbWidth: number;
    thumbHeight: number;
  }[] = [];

  for (const printing of card.printings) {
    const key = faceKeyFor(printing.image_url);
    if (key === null || seen.has(key)) continue;
    seen.add(key);

    const orientation = orientationOf({
      playedHorizontally: card.played_horizontally,
      rotationDegrees: printing.image_rotation_degrees,
    });
    const normalBox = boxFor("normal", orientation);
    const thumbBox = boxFor("thumb", orientation);

    entries.push({
      key,
      id: printing.id,
      edition: printing.edition,
      setName: setName(printing.set_id),
      setCode: printing.set_id,
      thumb: faceUrl(key, "thumb"),
      normal: faceUrl(key, "normal"),
      width: normalBox.width,
      height: normalBox.height,
      thumbWidth: thumbBox.width,
      thumbHeight: thumbBox.height,
    });
  }

  /*
    DISAMBIGUATED ONLY WHERE IT IS NEEDED, AND ONLY WHERE IT WORKS.

    Set and collector number identify a printing almost always — but not always:
    Head Jab's Welcome to Rathe entry is Alpha AND Unlimited, two different
    pieces of art under one number, and labelling both "Welcome to Rathe ·
    WTR098" is exactly the useless repetition naming them by set was meant to
    fix. The edition is appended only to the groups that collide, so an ordinary
    tile stays a set and a number.

    THE SECOND CONDITION IS NEW, AND IT IS THE ONE THAT WAS WRONG. A collision
    was assumed to be an edition difference, and it is not always: Arakni's
    HNT264 collides with itself because upstream publishes a front and a back
    face under one number, both edition `N`. The old rule appended the edition
    to both — the SAME edition, to both — which distinguished nothing and, since
    `N` decodes to "No specified edition (used for promos, non-set releases,
    etc.)", spent sixty characters saying so twice under two thumbnails.

    So the edition is appended only when the colliding printings actually differ
    by it. Where they do not, the tiles keep the plain number and are told apart
    by the thing that genuinely differs — the picture, which is what this
    control is for.
  */
  const editionsPerNumber = new Map<string, Set<string>>();
  for (const entry of entries) {
    const key = `${entry.setCode}/${entry.id}`;
    const seenEditions = editionsPerNumber.get(key) ?? new Set<string>();
    seenEditions.add(entry.edition);
    editionsPerNumber.set(key, seenEditions);
  }

  for (const entry of entries) {
    if ((editionsPerNumber.get(`${entry.setCode}/${entry.id}`)?.size ?? 0) > 1) {
      entry.id = `${entry.id} · ${editionName(entry.edition)}`;
    }
  }

  return entries;
})();

/**
 * The card still needs a face when no printing publishes art — four printings
 * in the corpus, and the picker has nothing to show for them.
 */
const facelessBox = boxFor("normal", page.face.orientation);

/**
 * Shown only when there is a choice to make. One printing is not a rail, it is
 * the same picture again.
 */

/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Pitch versions                                                              */
/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/**
 * The red, yellow and blue versions of a card, as tabs.
 *
 * A PLAYER CALLS THESE ONE CARD, AND THE DATASET CALLS THEM THREE. Upstream
 * gives each pitch version its own `unique_id`, its own printed text and its own
 * legality, because mechanically they are distinct — "Head Jab" red and "Head
 * Jab" blue do different things. But nobody at a table says "I'm playing the
 * pitch-2 Head Jab" as though naming a different card; they say Head Jab and
 * hold up a yellow one. So the versions belong on ONE page, switched, rather
 * than on three pages a reader has to find separately or on an index page that
 * makes them choose before showing them anything.
 *
 * THE TABS ARE REAL LINKS, NOT A SCRIPTED WIDGET, and that is the whole reason
 * this works on a static site. Each version keeps its own permanent URL, so a
 * tab is something you can paste; the switch works with scripting off; and there
 * is no state to lose. `docs/PLAN.md`: "Every view is a URL." A JavaScript tab
 * panel would have taken three addressable documents and made them one
 * unaddressable one, which is the opposite of what this project is for.
 *
 * The jewel carries the colour and the label carries the number, which is the
 * pitch contract from `docs/DESIGN.md` — the numeral is the primary channel and
 * colour is the redundant one, so a tab strip is legible to a reader who cannot
 * tell red from yellow. No colour NAMES are invented here: "Pitch 2" is the
 * vocabulary `variantSuffix` already uses in every link to a variant.
 */
/**
 * No-pitch sorts last, matching `byPitch` in `cards.ts`, so a card and its
 * token variant do not swap places between the tab strip and the URL scheme.
 *
 * At module scope because it captures nothing — the same rule
 * `unicorn(consistent-function-scoping)` enforces on `pitchRank` in `cards.ts`,
 * and for the same reason: a comparator that rebuilds a closure on every one of
 * its O(n log n) calls is what the rule exists to catch.
 */
function pitchRank(pitch: number): number {
  return pitch === 0 ? 4 : pitch;
}

const versions = [
  { pitch: page.pitch, href: page.href, current: true },
  ...page.variants.map((variant) => ({
    pitch: variant.pitch,
    href: variant.href,
    current: false,
  })),
].toSorted((a, b) => pitchRank(a.pitch) - pitchRank(b.pitch));

const showVersions = versions.length > 1;

/**
 * `?pitch=N`, as an ALIAS that navigates rather than a switch that renders.
 *
 * The asymmetry with `?printing=` is not an inconsistency, it is the difference
 * between the two things. Printings of one card differ ONLY by image, so one
 * document with a selection is exactly right. Pitch versions are different
 * cards — different printed text, different stats, different legality — and
 * each already has its own statically-rendered page at `/card/<name>-<pitch>`.
 *
 * Rendering `?pitch=2` in place would mean shipping every version's text,
 * stats, keywords and six format verdicts to every card page so a script could
 * swap them, and would leave a reader with scripting off, and every crawler,
 * looking at the wrong card. Navigating to the page that already exists costs
 * one redirect and keeps the static document canonical.
 *
 * So the param works — it is linkable, which is what was asked for — and it
 * resolves to the address that was already the answer.
 */
const pitchTargets = JSON.stringify(
  Object.fromEntries(versions.map((version) => [String(version.pitch), version.href])),
);

---

<nav class="lineage" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
  <ol class="crumbs">
    <li><a href="/">Optfall</a></li>
    <li><a href="/search">Cards</a></li>
    {
      page.disambiguated && (
        <li>
          <a href={nameHref}>{card.name}</a>
        </li>
      )
    }
    <li><span class="crumb-current" aria-current="page">{page.label}</span></li>
  </ol>
</nav>

{/*
  Inline because it is a redirect rather than a component: an island would ship
  a runtime to do what four lines of navigation do, and it has to run before the
  reader reads anything.
*/}
<script is:inline define:vars={{ pitchTargets }}>
  {
    const wanted = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get("pitch");
    if (wanted !== null) {
      const target = JSON.parse(pitchTargets)[wanted];
      // Only when it names a DIFFERENT version — `?pitch=1` on the pitch-1 page
      // must not redirect to itself forever.
      if (target && target !== window.location.pathname.replace(/\/$/, "")) {
        const url = new URL(target, window.location.origin);
        // Everything else the reader asked for travels with them.
        for (const [key, value] of new URLSearchParams(window.location.search)) {
          if (key !== "pitch") url.searchParams.set(key, value);
        }
        window.location.replace(url.href);
      }
    }
  }
</script>

<article>
  {/*
    TWO COLUMNS, AND THIS TIME THERE IS ROOM FOR THEM.

    `docs/SCRYFALL-GAP.md` §6a asked for this shape and the markup has claimed
    to have it since: everything answering "what is this card" beside the face,
    the apparatus below. It never rendered. `main` was capped at the prose
    measure on every page in the site, the face is `card.face.normal`, and the
    facts column asked for a face-width basis on top of that — so the line was
    unsatisfiable at every viewport the site could be opened at, the facts
    wrapped underneath every time, and what shipped was the eight stacked
    sections the redesign was written to replace. `BaseLayout`'s `width="wide"`
    is the missing half; see the prop for why the number is a sum.

    It still collapses to one column, and the rule is now a real one rather than
    an accident: two columns when the line can seat a face beside a column at
    least as wide as a face, one below that.
  */}
  <div class="card-page">
    <div class="face-column">
      {/*
        THE PICKER IS AN ISLAND, and it is the only one on this page. §5.2 rules
        out live updating for SEARCH — results must not re-rank under a cursor,
        because the URL is the product. A printing picker is the opposite case:
        it changes which image is shown and nothing else. No query, no ranking,
        nothing a reader would want to paste that the card's own URL does not
        already carry.
      */}
      {
        printings.length > 0 ? (
          <PrintingPicker
            printings={printings}
            alt={faceAlt}
            label={page.label}
            client:load
          />
        ) : (
          <CardFace
            src={placeholderUrl(page.face.orientation)}
            alt={faceAlt}
            width={facelessBox.width}
            height={facelessBox.height}
            loading="eager"
          />
        )
      }
    </div>

    <div class="facts-column">
      {/*
        THE TAB STRIP IS ABOVE THE PANEL, NOT IN IT.

        It was a band for a while, and it was the one band that is not on the
        card. The panel below is a mirror of a printed object; a control for
        choosing WHICH printed object it mirrors belongs outside the frame,
        the same way the printings picker sits outside the face rather than on
        top of it.
      */}
      {
        showVersions && (
          <nav class="versions" aria-label={`Pitch versions of ${card.name}`}>
            <ul class="version-tabs">
              {versions.map((version) => (
                <li>
                  {version.current ? (
                    /*
                      The current tab is not a link. An anchor pointing at the
                      page you are already on is a control that does nothing,
                      and `aria-current` on a link still leaves it in the tab
                      order as a dead end.
                    */
                    <span class="version-tab current" aria-current="page">
                      <PitchJewel value={version.pitch} size="sm" />
                      <span class="version-label">
                        {version.pitch === 0 ? "No pitch" : `Pitch ${version.pitch}`}
                      </span>
                    </span>
                  ) : (
                    <a class="version-tab" href={version.href}>
                      <PitchJewel value={version.pitch} size="sm" />
                      <span class="version-label">
                        {version.pitch === 0 ? "No pitch" : `Pitch ${version.pitch}`}
                      </span>
                    </a>
                  )}
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </nav>
        )
      }

      {/*
        THE PANEL IS THE CARD, AND IT IS LAID OUT LIKE ONE.

        Not like Scryfall's panel, which is where this started: Scryfall puts
        the mana cost on the name line and power/toughness on the type line,
        because that is where Magic prints them. Flesh and Blood prints its
        stats somewhere else, so copying the arrangement rather than the idea
        would have produced a panel that mirrors the wrong game.

        The idea is that the panel reads in the order the card reads. On a Flesh
        and Blood card that is:

            <pitch>   NAME   <cost>
            <card text>
            <attack>  <type>  <defence>
            <artist>

        — pitch struck into the top-left corner, cost into the top-right, the
        text box between them, the bottom bar carrying attack left, type centre
        and defence right, and the credit in the margin under all of it. Every
        band below is one of those lines, in that order, on those sides.

        What was here before the redesign was six sibling `<section>`s — Stats,
        Printed text, Governed by, Legality — each with the same uppercase micro
        heading and the same margin: a stack of equally-weighted apparatus with
        the card's own text as one entry in it. Nothing said "this part IS the
        card and the rest is about it". The bands say it in layout, and now they
        say it in the card's own arrangement.

        Everything below the panel — legality, the rules join, the attribute
        vocabularies — is commentary ON the card and looks like it, which is
        what lets the legality verdict stay above the fold without competing
        with the text.
      */}
      <BevelledPlate emphasis="flat">
        <div class="panel">
          <header class="band band-title">
            {
              page.pitch === 0 ? (
                /* Keeps the name in the centre column when there is no jewel to
                   hold the left corner — a hero has no pitch, and its name is
                   still centred on the card. */
                <span class="corner-empty" aria-hidden="true" />
              ) : (
                <dl class="badges badges-start">
                  <div class="badge">
                    <dt>Pitch</dt>
                    <dd>
                      <PitchJewel value={page.pitch} size="lg" />
                    </dd>
                  </div>
                </dl>
              )
            }

            {/*
              THE BARE NAME, NOT THE DISAMBIGUATED LABEL. Everywhere else in the
              product a link to this card renders `page.label` — "Head Jab (pitch
              2)" — because 900 names belong to more than one card and two anchors
              that differ only in where they point are a WCAG 2.4.4 failure. Here
              the tab strip immediately above carries that distinction, and
              visibly, so repeating it in the heading would say the same thing
              twice and make the three versions read as three cards.
            */}
            <h1 class="card-name">{card.name}</h1>

            {
              costStat === undefined ? (
                <span class="corner-empty" aria-hidden="true" />
              ) : (
                <dl class="badges badges-end">
                  <div class="badge">
                    <dt>{costStat.label}</dt>
                    <dd>
                      <StatGlyph kind="cost" value={costStat.value} />
                    </dd>
                  </div>
                </dl>
              )
            }
          </header>

          {/*
            THE TEXT IS NO LONGER IN A PLATE INSIDE A SECTION INSIDE A COLUMN.

            It used to be wrapped in its own raised `BevelledPlate` under an
            `<h2>Printed text</h2>` — a box, in a box, under a label. That was
            the right instinct (the text is the most important thing here) in
            the wrong register: the emphasis was spent on a container, and by
            then the container was one of six.

            The panel is the box now, so the text simply IS the widest band in
            it, set a step larger than everything around it. The heading stays
            for a screen reader, which navigates by headings and would otherwise
            arrive at unannounced prose.
          */}
          <div class="band band-oracle">
            <h2 class="visually-hidden" id="printed-text">Printed text</h2>
            {
              text === "" ? (
                <p class="void">This card has no rules text in the published dataset.</p>
              ) : (
                <PrintedText text={card.functional_text} uid={page.slug} />
              )
            }
          </div>

          {
            soleFlavour !== undefined && (
              <div class="band band-flavour">
                <h2 class="visually-hidden" id="flavour-text">Flavour text</h2>
                <p class="flavour-text">{soleFlavour}</p>
              </div>
            )
          }

          {/*
            THE BOTTOM BAR: attack left, type centre, defence right.

            This is the line along the foot of a Flesh and Blood card, and the
            reason the stats are split across two `<dl>`s rather than sitting in
            one cluster. It is also why they are BELOW the text rather than
            above it — the earlier arrangement put them on the type line under
            the name, which is Magic's layout, and a panel that mirrors the
            wrong game's card is a worse artefact than one that mirrors none.

            Still definition lists, and the argument for that has not changed:
            cost, power and defence are term-and-value data, and marking them up
            as anything else would be a lie for a layout's sake. What has gone is
            the `<h2>Stats</h2>` that used to head them — a heading's worth of
            apparatus over what the card carries in its corners. 13 cards
            genuinely have a printed power of 0 and still get an entry saying 0.
          */}
          <div class="band band-bar">
            {
              startStats.length === 0 ? (
                <span class="corner-empty" aria-hidden="true" />
              ) : (
                <dl class="badges badges-start">
                  {startStats.map((stat) => (
                    <div class="badge">
                      <dt>{stat.label}</dt>
                      <dd>
                        {/*
                          THE SHAPE SAYS WHICH STAT IT IS, as docs/DESIGN.md
                          always described the card face — cost in a hexagonal
                          plate, power and defence in chamfered ones. The numeral
                          stays the primary channel and the label stays visible,
                          so the silhouette is the third redundant carrier rather
                          than the only one.
                        */}
                        {stat.kind === null ? stat.value : <StatGlyph kind={stat.kind} value={stat.value} />}
                      </dd>
                    </div>
                  ))}
                </dl>
              )
            }

            <p class="type-line">{typeLine === "" ? "Flesh and Blood card" : typeLine}</p>

            {
              endStats.length === 0 ? (
                <span class="corner-empty" aria-hidden="true" />
              ) : (
                <dl class="badges badges-end">
                  {endStats.map((stat) => (
                    <div class="badge">
                      <dt>{stat.label}</dt>
                      <dd>
                        {stat.kind === null ? stat.value : <StatGlyph kind={stat.kind} value={stat.value} />}
                      </dd>
                    </div>
                  ))}
                </dl>
              )
            }

            {
              !hasStats && (
                <p class="void">No printed cost, power, defence, life, intellect or arcane value.</p>
              )
            }
          </div>

          {/*
            THE FOOT OF THE PANEL, IN THE FINE-PRINT REGISTER — the line Scryfall
            puts under the flavour: who drew it, and which printings exist. Both
            are read straight off the record. The printing count is a link rather
            than a number because the table it names is on this same page, and a
            count that cannot take you to what it counted is decoration.
          */}
          <footer class="band band-credits">
            <p class="credit">
              {/*
                RARITY LEADS THE CREDIT LINE, initial in a bubble and the rest of
                the word beside it — "(M)ajestic" as one word rather than a chip
                and a caption. The letter is the form the card itself prints in
                its bottom margin; the rest of the word is there because a letter
                alone is a lookup, and this is a reference work.

                The colour is a grouping and not a claim — nothing upstream
                publishes a colour for a rarity. See `color.rarity.*`.
              */}
              {rarities.map((rarity) => (
                <span class="rarity" data-rarity={rarity.slug}>
                  <span class="rarity-mark" aria-hidden="true">{rarity.initial}</span>
                  {/* The full word for a screen reader, which must not be read
                      the initial and the remainder as two separate things. */}
                  <span class="visually-hidden">{rarity.name}</span>
                  <span class="rarity-rest" aria-hidden="true">{rarity.rest}</span>
                </span>
              ))}
              {
                artists.length === 0
                  ? "No artist is credited in the published dataset."
                  : `Illustrated by ${artists.join(", ")}`
              }
            </p>
            <p class="credit">
              <a href="#printings">{printingCount} printing{printingCount === 1 ? "" : "s"}</a>
            </p>
          </footer>
        </div>
      </BevelledPlate>

      {/*
        REPRODUCED *AND* INTERPRETED, WITH BOTH ON THE PAGE.

        This note used to say the opposite: "Optfall has no published table
        mapping those markers to words — so substituting any would be
        inventing a vocabulary and calling it the card's text." The premise
        was true of the card corpus and false of this repository. The
        Comprehensive Rules — already committed, already parsed, already what
        every keyword on this page cites — defines all of them at 1.12.4a–h.

        So rendering them is not an interpretation, it is the same join this
        site already makes between a keyword and its rule. "Raw text" keeps
        upstream's bytes one click away, because a join is a claim and a
        reference work should let you check it.

        It sits BELOW the panel rather than inside it, with the rest of the
        apparatus: it is a note about how the text was rendered, not part of
        the card, and the panel holds only what the card itself carries.
      */}

      {/*
        LEGALITY IS THE FIRST THING UNDER THE PANEL, which is where Scryfall
        puts its legality grid and — separately — where this project's own
        argument puts it. `docs/SCRYFALL-GAP.md` §3: "our legality table is
        already better than Scryfall's … keep this, and put it on the card page
        above the fold — it is the differentiator that is already finished."
        It reads as a grid now rather than as six stacked subsections. Six
        formats, each a serif `<h3>` over its own pills with a hairline above,
        ran to most of a screen on its own and pushed the rules join out of
        sight; the verdict for one format is a name and one or two pills, which
        is a row, and rows belong side by side. Nothing is lost in the
        compression — the multi-state list, the `since` dates and the
        "we do not know" case all still render, because those are the parts that
        make this table worth having.
      */}
      <section class="apparatus" aria-labelledby="legality">
        <h2 class="apparatus-heading" id="legality">Legality</h2>

        {/*
          THE SCOPE SENTENCE IS GONE — "present day only, confirmed <date>. A
          `since` date is when the current state began, not a history." It was
          true, careful, and read by nobody: a paragraph of qualification above
          six one-line verdicts. The date it carried is in Source, which is
          where a fact about the corpus belongs, and the `since` dates in the
          grid still say what they are.
        */}
        <ul class="formats">
          {
            page.verdicts.map((verdict) => (
              <li class="format">
                <h3 class="format-name" id={`format-${verdict.format.id}`}>
                  {verdict.format.name}
                </h3>

                {verdict.unknown ? (
                  /*
                    THE SAME REFUSAL, SAID ONCE INSTEAD OF SIX TIMES. This cell
                    used to carry the whole two-sentence explanation, and a card
                    outside four formats therefore carried four identical
                    paragraphs — 60 words of the page's most careful reasoning,
                    repeated until it read as boilerplate and stopped being read
                    at all. The row says what is true of THIS format; the
                    sentence beneath the grid says what that means, verbatim,
                    for however many rows say it.

                    It is deliberately not a `StatePill`. A pill here would have
                    to name a state, and the entire point is that there is no
                    state to name — "Not in format" is a claim upstream did not
                    make and this project will not make for it.
                  */
                  <p class="unknown">No flag published</p>
                ) : (
                  <ul class="states">
                    {verdict.states.map((state) => (
                      <li class="state">
                        <StatePill tone={state.tone} label={state.label} />
                        {state.since !== null && (
                          <span class="since">
                            since <time datetime={state.since}>{state.since}</time>
                          </span>
                        )}
                      </li>
                    ))}
                  </ul>
                )}

                {verdict.affectsFullCycle && verdict.format.id === "living-legend" && (
                  <p class="scope">Upstream records this restriction as affecting the full cycle of cards sharing this name.</p>
                )}

                {/*
                  THE UPSTREAM FLAGS ARE NO LONGER RENDERED, and the argument
                  for them is worth recording rather than deleting.

                  They existed for auditability: "the tool is auditable rather
                  than merely assertive, which is what matters when the
                  commercially-backed incumbent ships incorrect flags". That
                  still matters. What changed is the observation that the row
                  was saying what the pills already said — `ll_banned: true`
                  beside a pill reading Banned, `ll_banned_start: 2024-07-08`
                  beside "since 2024-07-08" — in upstream's key names rather
                  than in words. It was a second rendering of the verdict, not
                  evidence for it.

                  Auditability moves to where PLAN.md already puts it: "Publish
                  the data, not just the site." The full flag set ships in the
                  openly-licensed corpus, which is the artefact a second tool
                  would check us against — and a reader who wants to see the
                  derivation reads the export rather than 36 rows of dashes.
                */}
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>

        {
          page.verdicts.some((verdict) => verdict.unknown) && (
            <p class="verify">
              Where no flag is published, Optfall has nothing to say about this card in that format. Upstream publishes only exclusions there, and none is set on this card.
            </p>
          )
        }
      </section>

      {
        keywordRules.length > 0 && (
          <section class="apparatus" aria-labelledby="governed-by">
            <h2 class="apparatus-heading" id="governed-by">Governed by</h2>
            <ul class="rules-list">
              {keywordRules.map((rule) => (
                <li class="rules-item">
                  <a class="rules-citation" href={hrefForNumber(rule.number)}>{rule.ruleId}</a>
                  <span class="rules-keyword">{rule.keyword}</span>
                  {rule.via === "family" && (
                    /*
                      SAID OUT LOUD, because it is a slightly weaker claim. The
                      rules define `Specialization` once and cards instantiate
                      it per hero, so "Briar Specialization is governed by the
                      Specialization rule" is true but is a resolution rather
                      than a direct hit — and a reference work should say which
                      kind of claim it is making.
                    */
                    <span class="rules-via">via the general rule</span>
                  )}
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
            <p class="verify">Matched exactly against the vocabulary the rules publish. A keyword the document does not define is absent rather than guessed at.</p>
          </section>
        )
      }

      {/*
        ATTRIBUTES COME UP INTO THE COLUMN, out of the full-width run below.
        This is Scryfall's "card info" block — the metadata that describes the
        card rather than the printing — and it belongs beside the face with the
        rest of what the card IS, not stranded between the printings table and
        the related-cards list where a reader has already left the card behind.
      */}
      {
        vocabulary.length > 0 && (
          <section class="apparatus" aria-labelledby="attributes">
            <h2 class="apparatus-heading" id="attributes">Attributes</h2>
            <dl class="attributes">
              {vocabulary.map(([label, values]) => (
                <div class="attribute">
                  <dt>{label}</dt>
                  <dd>
                    <ul class="tags">
                      {values.map((value) => (
                        <li class="tag">{value}</li>
                      ))}
                    </ul>
                  </dd>
                </div>
              ))}
              {card.played_horizontally && (
                <div class="attribute">
                  <dt>Orientation</dt>
                  <dd>Played horizontally</dd>
                </div>
              )}
            </dl>
          </section>
        )
      }
    </div>
  </div>
</article>

<OrnamentalRule label="Printings" />

<section class="apparatus" aria-labelledby="printings">
  <h2 class="apparatus-heading" id="printings">Printings</h2>
  <p class="scope">
    {printingCount} printing{printingCount === 1 ? "" : "s"}. The collector number
    is the citable identity of a printing, which is why this table lists every
    one of them — including the printings with no published art, which the
    picker beside the face cannot show.
  </p>

  <div class="scroller">
    <table class="printings">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">Every published printing of {page.label}</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Number</th>
          <th scope="col">Set</th>
          <th scope="col">Rarity</th>
          <th scope="col">Edition</th>
          <th scope="col">Foiling</th>
          <th scope="col">Artist</th>
          <th scope="col">Other face</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          page.printings.map(({ printing, otherFace }) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="collector">{printing.id}</th>
              {/*
                IN WORDS, WITH THE CODE KEPT BESIDE IT. Until now this table
                printed upstream's storage letters — R, N, S — which is a
                database dump rather than a reference work. The name is what a
                reader wants; the code is what a printing is CITED by, so both
                are here and the code stays in the mono voice that means
                citable. An unknown code falls back to itself rather than to a
                blank, so a set Optfall has not re-synced reads as unnamed
                rather than as missing.
              */}
              <td>
                <a href={hrefForSet(printing.set_id)}>{setName(printing.set_id)}</a>
                <span class="code-hint">{printing.set_id}</span>
              </td>
              <td>{printing.rarity === "" ? "—" : rarityName(printing.rarity)}</td>
              <td>{printing.edition === "" ? "—" : editionName(printing.edition)}</td>
              <td>{printing.foiling === "" ? "—" : foilingName(printing.foiling)}</td>
              <td>{printing.artists.length === 0 ? "—" : printing.artists.join(", ")}</td>
              <td>
                {otherFace === null ? (
                  "—"
                ) : (
                  <a href={otherFace.href}>{otherFace.label}</a>
                )}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  {/*
    ONLY WHERE THE PRINTINGS DISAGREE. A card whose every printing carries the
    same flavour has that flavour shown once, in the panel, where a reader looks
    for it — see `soleFlavour`. Repeating it here under a collector number would
    be the same words twice, the second time implying a distinction between
    printings that does not exist.
  */}
  {
    flavours.length > 1 && (
      <div class="flavour">
        <h3 class="apparatus-heading" id="flavour">Flavour text, by printing</h3>
        <dl class="attributes">
          {page.printings
            .filter(({ printing }) => printing.flavor_text.trim() !== "")
            .map(({ printing }) => (
              <div class="attribute">
                <dt class="collector">{printing.id}</dt>
                <dd class="flavour-text">{printing.flavor_text}</dd>
              </div>
            ))}
        </dl>
      </div>
    )
  }
</section>

{
  relatedShown.length > 0 && (
    <>
      <OrnamentalRule label="Related cards" />
      <section class="apparatus" aria-labelledby="related">
        <h2 class="apparatus-heading" id="related">Related cards</h2>
        {relatedShown.map(([label, blurb, links]) => (
          <div class="related">
            <h3 class="related-name">{label}</h3>
            <p class="scope">{blurb}</p>
            <ul class="links">
              {links.map((link) => (
                <li class="link">
                  <PitchJewel value={link.pitch} size="sm" />
                  {/*
                    `link.label`, never `link.name`. Two cards on this list can
                    share a name; the label is what makes the two anchors
                    distinguishable to somebody reading the links alone.
                  */}
                  <a href={link.href}>{link.label}</a>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </div>
        ))}
      </section>
    </>
  )
}

{/*
  THE PROVENANCE FOLDS. It is seven rows of hashes, URLs and a pinned commit —
  the auditability promise, and the thing almost nobody opens. It stayed
  expanded on the argument that a claim whose evidence needs a click is a claim
  being asserted; that argument belongs to the LEGALITY verdict, which is a
  claim about a card, and it does not extend to the corpus envelope, which is a
  claim about a file and is identical on all 4,941 pages.

  Closed by default, and one click from the reader who wants it. The rights
  line stays outside the fold, because it has to accompany the page rather than
  be available from it.
*/}
<OrnamentalRule label="Source" />

<section class="apparatus" aria-labelledby="source">
  <details class="source-fold">
    <summary class="source-summary"><h2 class="apparatus-heading" id="source">Source</h2></summary>
  <BevelledPlate emphasis="sunken">
    <dl class="provenance">
      <dt>Upstream</dt>
      <dd><a class="source-url" href={`https://github.com/${CORPUS.source.repository}`}>{CORPUS.source.repository}</a></dd>

      <dt>File</dt>
      <dd><code>{CORPUS.source.path}</code></dd>

      <dt>Pinned commit</dt>
      <dd><code class="hash">{CORPUS.source.commit}</code></dd>

      <dt>Last confirmed</dt>
      <dd><time datetime={LAST_CONFIRMED}>{LAST_CONFIRMED}</time></dd>

      <dt>Retrieved from</dt>
      <dd><a class="source-url" href={CORPUS.source.url}>{CORPUS.source.url}</a></dd>

      <dt>File SHA-256</dt>
      <dd><code class="hash">{CORPUS.source.sha256}</code></dd>

      <dt>This card</dt>
      <dd><code>{card.unique_id}</code></dd>
    </dl>
  </BevelledPlate>
  <p class="verify">Card {page.ordinal} of {CORPUS.counts.cards}. Fetch that URL, hash it, compare. Every value here is read from those bytes by deterministic code — no language model touches this corpus.</p>
  </details>
  <p class="legal">{CORPUS.rights}</p>
</section>

<style>
  /* -- The face and its printings rail ------------------------------------- */

  /*
    Layout only. `CardFace` owns everything about how a face is drawn, including
    the copyright line it will not let a caller omit; this places it and nothing
    more.
  */
  /*
    THE TWO-COLUMN FOLD, WITH NO BREAKPOINT — and that is deliberate rather
    than clever. There is not one width media query anywhere else in this
    repository: the system holds pages together with intrinsic sizing, and a
    chosen pixel width would be the first arbitrary number in a codebase whose
    lint rule exists to keep arbitrary numbers out. `check:tokens` refuses a raw
    length here, which is the rule doing its job.

    So the columns wrap when they stop fitting rather than when a designer
    guessed they would. WHAT WAS WRONG WAS THE THRESHOLD, NOT THE TECHNIQUE.
    The facts column asked for `card.face.normal` of preferred room beside a
    face that is itself `card.face.normal` wide — two faces and a gutter of
    hypothetical width, inside a `main` that every page in the site capped at
    the prose measure, which is narrower than the two faces alone. Flex wraps
    on hypothetical size, before shrinking is considered, so the line was over
    budget at every viewport this site can be opened at and the fold never once
    rendered. Two columns, in the markup, for months, always stacked.

    `BaseLayout`'s `width="wide"` gives the line room; the basis below now
    states a rule that can actually be met or missed: TWO COLUMNS WHEN THE PAGE
    CAN SEAT A FACE BESIDE A COLUMN AT LEAST AS WIDE AS A FACE, one below that.
    The `min(…, 100%)` is what keeps the unmet case from overflowing a phone
    instead of wrapping on it.
  */
  .card-page {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: var(--of-space-loosest);
  }

  .face-column {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--of-space-base);
    /* Asks for a face and will give some of it back before it wraps — the
       image is `max-inline-size: 100%` inside, so a squeezed column scales the
       face down rather than clipping it. */
    flex: 0 1 var(--of-card-face-normal);
    min-inline-size: min(var(--of-card-face-thumb), 100%);
  }

  /*
    NO `position: sticky` ON THE FACE, and it was tried. Following the reader
    down the legality list is genuinely nice on a wide screen — and the same
    declaration, once the columns have wrapped, pins the face to the top of a
    phone screen and scrolls the entire card underneath it. Turning it off for
    the wrapped case needs the breakpoint this layout exists to avoid, and a
    behaviour that is pleasant on a desktop and hostile on a phone is not worth
    a media query's worth of arbitrary number.
  */
  .facts-column {
    /* `min-inline-size: 0` because a flex item's default `auto` minimum refuses
       to shrink below its content, which a long card name would otherwise use
       to force a horizontal scroll. */
    flex: 1 1 min(var(--of-card-face-normal), 100%);
    min-inline-size: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    /* Tightened from `looser`. Scryfall runs its right column dense — the
       panel, the legality grid and the rulings sit close enough to be read as
       one document — and three of the paragraphs that used to need air here
       have been deleted. */
    gap: var(--of-space-loose);
  }

  /* -- The panel: the card, as an object ----------------------------------- */

  /*
    BANDS, DIVIDED BY HAIRLINES, IN THE ORDER THE PRINTED CARD PUTS THEM.

    The plate around this is a `BevelledPlate`, so the border, the bevel and the
    padding are the component's; everything here is the division of what sits
    inside it. A band is separated from the one above by the same hairline the
    rest of the system separates regions with — no boxes, no shadows, no second
    background — which is why the panel reads as one object with parts rather
    than as four stacked cards.

    The rules are inset by the plate's own padding rather than running edge to
    edge. Full-bleed would mean reaching into `BevelledPlate` to zero its
    padding and re-adding it per band, and a component's box is the component's
    business — "compose, never restyle" is the project rule, and an inset rule
    is a smaller cost than breaking it.
  */
  .panel {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  .band + .band {
    margin-block-start: var(--of-space-tight);
    padding-block-start: var(--of-space-tight);
    border-block-start: var(--of-ornament-rule-width) solid var(--of-color-rule);
  }

  /*
    THE TWO CORNER BANDS, EACH A THREE-COLUMN GRID.

    A corner, the centred thing, the other corner — which is the geometry of
    both the top of a Flesh and Blood card (pitch, name, cost) and the bottom of
    one (attack, type, defence). The columns are `auto 1fr auto` so the corners
    take exactly what their plates need and the middle takes the rest; the name
    and the type line are centred inside that middle column rather than by the
    grid, so a corner that is absent cannot slide them off-axis.

    A corner that is absent renders `.corner-empty` rather than nothing, because
    an omitted grid item would let the next one take its column and put the
    cost where the pitch should be.

    `align-items: center` on the bar and `start` on the title: a name wrapping
    to three lines should grow downward past a jewel that stays at the top, and
    a one-line type line should sit level with the plates beside it.
  */
  .band-title,
  .band-bar {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
    gap: var(--of-space-base);
  }

  .band-title {
    align-items: start;
  }

  .band-bar {
    align-items: center;
  }

  /*
    Serif — the voice assigned to names — and the one size on this page that
    has to be fluid.

    THE NAME IS THE THING THAT BREAKS THE MIRROR ON A PHONE. It sits in the
    middle column between two corners, so at `type.size.display` on a phone-width
    viewport "Aether Flare" wraps to two lines and shoulders the jewel and the
    cost plate to the edges of a band that is mostly gutter. The printed card is
    no wider and puts all three on one line — it just sets the name
    smaller when the card is smaller.

    `clamp` between two steps of the scale does the same thing, and it does it
    without the breakpoint this layout has spent its whole life avoiding: the
    name is never smaller than `title`, never larger than `display`, and in
    between it tracks the viewport. The middle term is the only `vw` in this
    file and it is doing the job a media query would otherwise be hired for.
  */
  .card-name {
    margin-block: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    text-align: center;
    font-size: clamp(var(--of-type-size-title), 4.5vw, var(--of-type-size-display));
  }

  /* Same argument, one step down the scale: the type line shares the bottom
     bar with up to three stat plates, and it is the only elastic thing in it. */
  .type-line {
    margin: 0;
    text-align: center;
    font-family: var(--of-type-family-serif);
    font-size: clamp(var(--of-type-size-base), 2.5vw, var(--of-type-size-large));
    color: var(--of-color-ink-muted);
  }

  /*
    The stat cluster. Still a `<dl>` — see the note in the markup — and still
    labelled, because the glyph's silhouette is the third redundant carrier of
    which stat it is rather than the only one.
  */
  .badges {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: var(--of-space-base);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .badges-end {
    justify-content: flex-end;
  }

  .badge {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--of-space-tightest);
  }

  .badge dt {
    font-family: var(--of-type-family-sans);
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-micro);
    letter-spacing: var(--of-type-tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--of-color-ink-faint);
  }

  .badge dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--of-type-family-sans);
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-title);
    color: var(--of-color-ink);
  }

  /*
    The foot of the panel: attribution and the printing count, in the fine-print
    register the card-image copyright line already established. Present, never
    loud — the same argument, and so the same treatment.
  */
  .band-credits {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--of-space-tight) var(--of-space-base);
  }

  .credit {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-micro);
    color: var(--of-color-ink-faint);
  }

  /* -- Rarity -------------------------------------------------------------- */

  .rarity {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    /* No gap: the bubble and the remainder are one word interrupted, not two
       elements set beside each other. */
    margin-inline-end: var(--of-space-tight);
  }

  /*
    The bubble. A disc, like the stat markers above it and for the same reason —
    the card prints its rarity as a letter in a coloured dot in the bottom
    margin, which is where this line sits.

    The `--rarity` custom property defaults to the muted ink of the line it is
    on, so a rarity upstream adds tomorrow renders as a plain grey bubble rather
    than as an unset `var()` and no background at all. Each known rarity
    overrides it below.
  */
  .rarity-mark {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    inline-size: var(--of-ornament-jewel-small);
    block-size: var(--of-ornament-jewel-small);
    clip-path: var(--of-ornament-cut-disc);
    background: var(--rarity, var(--of-color-ink-faint));
    color: var(--rarity-ink, var(--of-color-ground));
    font-weight: var(--of-type-weight-bold);
    line-height: 1;
  }

  .rarity-rest {
    color: var(--of-color-ink-muted);
  }

  .rarity[data-rarity="common"] {
    --rarity: var(--of-color-rarity-common);
    --rarity-ink: var(--of-color-rarity-common-ink);
  }

  .rarity[data-rarity="rare"] {
    --rarity: var(--of-color-rarity-rare);
    --rarity-ink: var(--of-color-rarity-rare-ink);
  }

  .rarity[data-rarity="super"] {
    --rarity: var(--of-color-rarity-super);
    --rarity-ink: var(--of-color-rarity-super-ink);
  }

  .rarity[data-rarity="majestic"] {
    --rarity: var(--of-color-rarity-majestic);
    --rarity-ink: var(--of-color-rarity-majestic-ink);
  }

  .rarity[data-rarity="legendary"] {
    --rarity: var(--of-color-rarity-legendary);
    --rarity-ink: var(--of-color-rarity-legendary-ink);
  }

  .rarity[data-rarity="fabled"] {
    --rarity: var(--of-color-rarity-fabled);
    --rarity-ink: var(--of-color-rarity-fabled-ink);
  }

  .rarity[data-rarity="token"] {
    --rarity: var(--of-color-rarity-token);
    --rarity-ink: var(--of-color-rarity-token-ink);
  }

  .rarity[data-rarity="basic"] {
    --rarity: var(--of-color-rarity-basic);
    --rarity-ink: var(--of-color-rarity-basic-ink);
  }

  .rarity[data-rarity="marvel"] {
    --rarity: var(--of-color-rarity-marvel);
    --rarity-ink: var(--of-color-rarity-marvel-ink);
  }

  .rarity[data-rarity="promo"] {
    --rarity: var(--of-color-rarity-promo);
    --rarity-ink: var(--of-color-rarity-promo-ink);
  }

  .code-hint {
    margin-inline-start: var(--of-space-tight);
    font-family: var(--of-type-family-sans);
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-micro);
    letter-spacing: var(--of-type-tracking-wide);
    color: var(--of-color-ink-faint);
  }

  /* -- Governed by ---------------------------------------------------------- */

  .rules-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--of-space-tight);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .rules-item {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--of-space-base);
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  /* Monospaced, because in this system that means you can paste it into an
     argument — which is the entire point of a citation. */
  .rules-citation {
    font-family: var(--of-type-family-sans);
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-small);
    letter-spacing: var(--of-type-tracking-wide);
    color: var(--of-color-accent);
  }

  .rules-keyword {
    font-family: var(--of-type-family-serif);
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-base);
    text-transform: capitalize;
  }

  .rules-via {
    font-family: var(--of-type-family-sans);
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-micro);
    letter-spacing: var(--of-type-tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--of-color-ink-faint);
  }

  /* -- Pitch version tabs --------------------------------------------------- */

  .versions {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--of-space-tight);
  }

  .version-tabs {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--of-space-tight);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    /* The strip sits on a rule rather than in a box: the system separates
       regions with hairlines, and a tab row is a region boundary by nature. */
    border-block-end: var(--of-ornament-rule-width) solid var(--of-color-rule);
  }

  .version-tab {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--of-space-tight);
    padding: var(--of-space-tight) var(--of-space-base);
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--of-color-ink-muted);
    /* Square, never rounded — stated rather than omitted, so a host stylesheet
       cannot round the one control this page owns. */
    border-radius: var(--of-bevel-radius);
    /* Every tab reserves the selected edge, so selecting one moves no pixels. */
    border-block-end: var(--of-bevel-width) solid transparent;
  }

  .version-tab:hover {
    color: var(--of-color-ink);
  }

  /*
    The current tab is marked by weight, ink AND a bevelled edge — three
    channels, because "which version am I reading" is the single most important
    fact on a page that shows one of three near-identical cards.
  */
  .version-tab.current {
    color: var(--of-color-ink);
    border-block-end-color: var(--of-color-accent);
  }

  .version-label {
    font-family: var(--of-type-family-sans);
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-micro);
    letter-spacing: var(--of-type-tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  /* -- The source fold ------------------------------------------------------
     The heading IS the summary, so the disclosure carries no second label and
     the marker sits against the same uppercase micro type every other section
     head uses.

    `display` IS LEFT ALONE, and that is the whole rule. A `<summary>` is
    `display: list-item`, and its disclosure triangle is the `::marker` of that
    list item — set `inline-flex` (as this did) and Chrome drops the marker
    entirely, leaving a fold with no affordance at all: a heading that happens
    to be clickable, which nobody clicks. The `<h2>` inside is made inline
    instead, which is enough to keep the triangle and the text on one line.
  */
  .source-summary {
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .source-summary .apparatus-heading {
    display: inline;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .source-fold[open] .source-summary {
    margin-block-end: var(--of-space-base);
  }

  /* Layout and voice assignment only. Every value is a token — no literal
     colour, no literal length — and no media query, because a breakpoint is a
     length the token layer has nothing to offer. Everything below wraps or
     scrolls instead. */

  /* -- Breadcrumb ---------------------------------------------------------- */

  .lineage {
    margin-block-end: var(--of-space-looser);
  }

  .crumbs {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--of-space-tight);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-small);
  }

  /* Generated content, so a separator never lands in copied text. */
  .crumbs li + li::before {
    content: "›";
    color: var(--of-color-ink-muted);
    margin-inline-end: var(--of-space-tight);
  }

  .crumb-current {
    color: var(--of-color-ink-muted);
  }

  /* -- Shared apparatus ---------------------------------------------------- */

  /*
    Inside the facts column the gap owns the rhythm, so a margin here would be
    added to it rather than instead of it. The margin stays for the full-width
    sections below the fold, which are siblings in the document rather than
    items in a flex column.
  */
  .apparatus {
    margin-block: var(--of-space-loose);
  }

  .facts-column > .apparatus {
    margin-block: 0;
  }

  /* A real heading, restyled rather than downgraded to a `<p>`: the level is
     what a screen reader navigates by, and the mono micro type is what keeps it
     from shouting over the card. Same idiom as the rules page. */
  .apparatus-heading {
    margin-block: 0 var(--of-space-base);
    font-family: var(--of-type-family-sans);
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-micro);
    font-weight: var(--of-type-weight-medium);
    letter-spacing: var(--of-type-tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--of-color-ink-muted);
  }

  .scope {
    margin-block: 0 var(--of-space-base);
    color: var(--of-color-ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-small);
    line-height: var(--of-type-leading-loose);
  }

  .void {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--of-color-ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-small);
  }

  /*
    THE RIGHTS NOTICE IS LEGALESE, NOT APPARATUS. It is 500 characters of
    licence text that has to be present and does not have to compete — the same
    argument the card-image copyright line settled, so it gets the same
    treatment: the smallest step in the scale, tight, faint.
  */
  .legal {
    margin-block-start: var(--of-space-base);
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-legal);
    letter-spacing: var(--of-type-tracking-tight);
    line-height: var(--of-type-leading-base);
    color: var(--of-color-ink-faint);
  }

  .verify {
    margin-block: var(--of-space-base) 0;
    color: var(--of-color-ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-micro);
    line-height: var(--of-type-leading-loose);
  }

  /* The printed marker sits beside its word, at the label’s own baseline.
     Rehomed from `.stat dt` onto `.badge dt` when the stat block became two
     bands of the panel — the symbols are the same fact in the same place, and
     the selector followed the markup rather than the markup being kept for the
     selector. */
  /* The letter chip that used to sit here beside each stat's word is gone. It
     paired the label with the inline marker — "Power {p}" — and earned its
     place while the marker was an abstract plate that had to be taught. The
     markers are the card's own disc and shield now, so the chip was teaching
     what the reader already recognised, twice per card, in the tightest line on
     the page. `GameSymbol` is unchanged and still renders inline in the text,
     which is the place a marker is met cold. */
  .badge dt {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--of-space-tighter);
  }

  /* -- Printed text -------------------------------------------------------- */

  /* The `.card-text` rule that lived here moved into `PrintedText.astro` along
     with the markup it styled. It is deleted rather than left behind: a scoped
     rule matching no element in its own component is dead weight that reads,
     to the next person, like the styling is still handled here. */

  /* -- Attributes ---------------------------------------------------------- */

  .attributes {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--of-space-base);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .attribute {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--of-space-base);
  }

  .attribute dt {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    font-family: var(--of-type-family-sans);
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-micro);
    letter-spacing: var(--of-type-tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--of-color-ink-muted);
  }

  .attribute dd {
    flex: 1 1 60%;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-small);
  }

  .tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--of-space-tight);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* Not a `StatePill`: a keyword is not a state, and the notch means "this
     carries state" everywhere else in the system. A hairline outline is the
     honest weight for a tag. */
  .tag {
    padding-block: var(--of-space-tightest);
    padding-inline: var(--of-space-tight);
    border: var(--of-bevel-width) solid var(--of-color-rule);
    border-radius: var(--of-bevel-radius);
    font-family: var(--of-type-family-sans);
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-micro);
    letter-spacing: var(--of-type-tracking-wide);
    color: var(--of-color-ink-muted);
  }

  /* -- Legality ------------------------------------------------------------ */

  /*
    A GRID OF ROWS, WHICH IS WHAT A LEGALITY TABLE IS.

    Each verdict is a format name and one or two pills — a row — and six rows
    stacked full-width with a serif heading apiece ran the length of a screen
    to say six short things. Two-up, they are one glance.

    The column minimum is a length the token layer cannot supply, so it is
    derived from one it can: one and a half thumbs is the width at which the
    longest format name this corpus has ("Classic Constructed") and the longest
    pill it can carry ("Living Legend") still sit on one line together. Below
    that the grid drops to one column on its own, which is the right behaviour
    in the wrapped layout and on a phone.
  */
  .formats {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(
      auto-fit,
      minmax(min(calc(var(--of-card-face-thumb) * 1.5), 100%), 1fr)
    );
    gap: var(--of-space-tight) var(--of-space-loose);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /*
    The name and the verdict on one line, pushed to opposite edges — so the
    pills form a readable column of their own down the right of each grid
    column, and a reader scanning for "is it banned anywhere" scans one edge
    rather than six paragraphs.
  */
  .format {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--of-space-tight);
    padding-block: var(--of-space-tighter);
    border-block-start: var(--of-ornament-rule-width) solid var(--of-color-rule);
  }

  /* Demoted from the serif large it used to carry. It is a row label now, not a
     subsection heading, and the pill beside it is the thing worth reading. */
  .format-name {
    margin-block: 0;
    font-family: var(--of-type-family-sans);
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-small);
    letter-spacing: var(--of-type-tracking-normal);
    color: var(--of-color-ink-muted);
  }

  .states {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    gap: var(--of-space-tight);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .state {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--of-space-tighter);
  }

  .since {
    font-family: var(--of-type-family-sans);
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-micro);
    letter-spacing: var(--of-type-tracking-wide);
    color: var(--of-color-ink-muted);
  }

  /* Not a pill and deliberately quiet: it is the absence of a claim, and it
     must not read as one of the states beside it. */
  .unknown {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--of-type-family-sans);
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-micro);
    letter-spacing: var(--of-type-tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--of-color-ink-faint);
  }

  /* The full-cycle note belongs to its row rather than to the grid, so it takes
     the whole of that row's width underneath the pills. */
  .format .scope {
    flex-basis: 100%;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-micro);
  }

  /* -- Printings ----------------------------------------------------------- */

  /* The table is the one thing on this page that can genuinely exceed the
     measure — 50 printings of seven columns — so it scrolls inside its own box
     rather than making the document scroll sideways. */
  .scroller {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  /*
    A FLOOR, SO WRAPPING DEGRADES INTO SCROLLING RATHER THAN INTO SHREDDING.

    Letting the cells wrap fixed the over-wide table, and then over-fixed it:
    given a phone and seven columns, the browser dutifully fits by breaking "No
    specified edition (used for promos, non-set releases, etc.)" one word per
    line, turning every row into a ten-line ribbon. Narrower is not the same as
    more readable.

    So the table is never allowed below one measure. On a wide page that floor is
    slack and the cells wrap where they like; on a phone the table exceeds its
    box and `.scroller` does what it was always there to do. A seven-column
    table on a phone has to give up SOMETHING, and a sideways scroll inside one
    box is a smaller loss than a column of single words.
  */
  .printings {
    inline-size: 100%;
    min-inline-size: var(--of-type-measure);
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-small);
  }

  /*
    NOWRAP WAS ON EVERY CELL, AND ONE CELL IS A SENTENCE.

    `N` decodes to "No specified edition (used for promos, non-set releases,
    etc.)" — upstream's own gloss, 60 characters of it, and it is the commonest
    edition in the corpus. Held on one line it made the Edition column wider than a
    phone and the whole table wider than two of them — a table two and a half
    screens across with the artist and the other face off the right of it.

    So cells wrap by default and only the identifiers hold their line: a
    collector number or a set code broken across two lines is no longer a
    citation, which is the thing nowrap was for. The table still scrolls inside
    `.scroller` when it has to — it just very rarely has to now.
  */
  .printings th,
  .printings td {
    padding-block: var(--of-space-tight);
    padding-inline-end: var(--of-space-base);
    text-align: start;
    vertical-align: baseline;
    border-block-end: var(--of-ornament-rule-width) solid var(--of-color-rule);
  }

  .printings thead th,
  .printings .collector,
  .printings .code-hint {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .printings thead th {
    font-family: var(--of-type-family-sans);
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-micro);
